course: biology
lesson: 194
title: Reading a dog: tails, play bows, growls and staying safe
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dog cannot say «I'm scared» or «let's play», but it says both all day long with its tail, ears, eyes, mouth and whole body.
explain: Watch two dogs in the park: one drops its front legs to the grass with its bottom in the air, and the other bounces off after it. That bow means «this is a game, it's all fun».
explain: The same body can also say «stop»: a tucked tail, a yawn, the whites of the eyes showing, a stiff body or a growl are polite warnings that come before any bite.
explain: A wagging tail is not always friendly; you have to read the whole dog, not one part, and if a dog is telling you it is uneasy, the kind and safe thing is to give it space.
fact: A loose, wide wag with a wiggly bottom is usually friendly, but a stiff, high tail that wags fast while the dog stares can mean the dog is tense and ready to act.
fact: The play bow, front legs down and bottom up, is an invitation to play, and dogs also use it to say «still a game» after a rough moment.
fact: A dog that yawns, licks its lips or looks away when the vet comes close is usually not tired but uneasy; these are called calming signals.
fact: A tail tucked between the legs means the dog is scared, and a frightened dog that is cornered may bite, so it needs space.
fact: When a hugged dog shows the whites of its eyes, often called whale eye, it is uncomfortable; most dogs do not like hugs, and many bites to children come from dogs they know at home.
fact: A dog rolling on its back may trust you or may be trying to calm a game down; if it goes stiff when touched, it does not want a tummy rub.
fact: A growl is a warning, not bad behaviour; a dog punished for growling may learn to skip the warning and bite without one.
fact: If a strange dog runs at you, the safest thing is to stand still like a tree, arms in, looking away, and not run or scream, because running can make it chase.
fact: Always ask the owner before stroking a dog, let it sniff your hand, and stroke its side or chest rather than reaching over its head, because a hand coming down over its head can feel like a threat.
fact: Dogs sniff each other's bottoms to say hello, because special scent glands there tell who the other dog is, a bit like reading an identity card.
fact: Fur standing up along a dog's back is called raised hackles; it shows the dog is very worked up, which can be fear, anger or just big excitement.
exercise: A dog wags its tail stiffly and high while staring hard. What could it mean? | The dog is tense and may be ready to act | The dog is surely happy and friendly, because every wag means joy | The dog is tired and wants a sleep | The dog is cold and shivering
exercise: Which kind of wag is usually friendly? | A loose wide wag with a wiggly bottom | A stiff high wag with a hard stare straight at your face | A tail held still and straight up | A tail tucked tightly under the tummy
exercise: A dog drops its front legs with its bottom in the air. What is it saying? | Let's play, this is a game | I am about to bite | I have hurt my back | I want to go to sleep
exercise: Why might a dog yawn when the vet comes close? | It is nervous and trying to calm things down | It is bored of the vet | It stayed up far too late the night before | It wants the vet to give it a treat from the jar on the desk
exercise: What are signals like yawning and lip licking called? | Calming signals | Hunger signals for food | Sleepy bedtime signals | Attack warning signals
exercise: A dog's tail is tucked between its legs. How does it feel? | Scared | Proud of itself | Hungry for dinner | Playful
exercise: Why should you never corner a frightened dog? | A scared dog with no way out may bite | It will fall asleep | It will run all the way home and hide under the bed | It likes being cornered, because corners make dogs feel safe
exercise: A dog shows the whites of its eyes while a child hugs it. What does it mean? | The dog is uncomfortable and wants to be let go | The dog loves the hug and is showing how happy it is | The dog is falling asleep in the child's arms | The dog is looking for a ball
exercise: A dog rolls onto its back and goes stiff when you touch its tummy. What should you do? | Stop, it does not want a rub | Rub harder, because it is asking for more | Pick it up | Tickle its feet
exercise: A dog growls when a child goes near its food bowl. What is the growl? | A warning to keep away | A sign the food has gone bad | A way of saying thank you | A sign the dog is feeling ill
exercise: What can happen if a dog is punished every time it growls? | It may learn to bite without warning | It stops being scared of anything at all | It learns to share its food | It forgets how to bark and growl for good
exercise: A strange dog runs towards a child in the park. What is the safest thing to do? | Stand still like a tree, arms in, and look away | Run away as fast as you can, screaming for help | Scream and wave your arms above your head to look big | Stare into its eyes
exercise: A girl asks a dog owner before stroking the dog. Was she right? | Yes, always ask the owner first | No, dogs never mind being stroked by strangers | No, she should ask the dog's vet instead | Yes, but only for big dogs that might bite
exercise: Where is a good place to stroke a dog you have just met? | On its side or chest after it sniffs your hand | Over the top of its head, with a few firm pats | On its tail | On its face near the eyes and the nose
exercise: Why do two dogs sniff each other's bottoms? | It is a dog hello, the smell tells who the other dog is | They are checking each other for fleas and ticks before they play | They want to start a fight | They are looking for food
exercise: What does it mean when the fur on a dog's back stands up? | It is very worked up, perhaps scared, angry or excited | It is always about to attack, so you should run | It is cold | It has just had a bath and its fur has dried the wrong way
test: «look, his tail's going like mad, he's friendly, I'm gonna pet him» but the dog is stiff and staring. What is right? | A stiff fast wag with a stare is not a friendly sign, leave him | Any wag means friendly, go on, he wants to be stroked right now | Tails only wag when dogs are happy | Pet him on the head quickly before he moves
test: «Bella keeps yawning at the vet, she's just sleepy lol» What is more likely? | She is nervous, and yawning is a calming signal | She stayed up all night | Yawning means she is hungry and wants her dinner | Dogs yawn only when they are bored, and the vet's is boring
test: «hug him tighter, he loves it» but the dog is turning away and showing the whites of its eyes. What should happen? | Let go and give the dog space | Hug him tighter so he calms down | Kiss him on the nose to say sorry | Hold his head still so he looks at you
test: «that dog growled at my little bro, smack it so it learns» What is right? | No, the growl is a warning; keep children away from its food bowl | Yes, smacking stops all bites, because the dog learns who is boss | Yes, then it will never growl again | No, just give it more food until it is too full to care
test: «a dog's running at us, LEG IT!!» What is the safer thing to do? | Stand still like a tree and look away | Run screaming to the gate as fast as you can | Throw your shoe at it to scare it away | Stare it down until it stops and turns round
test: In a cartoon a dog is happy every time its tail wags. What is true of real dogs? | A wag can mean happy, tense or unsure, so read the whole dog | Every single wag is happy, just like in the cartoons | Real dogs never wag their tails | Wagging always means anger and a bite coming soon
test: «ew why's he sniffing that dog's butt, gross» What is the real reason? | It is a dog hello, the scent tells who the other dog is | He wants a fight and is looking for a soft place to bite | He is looking for leftovers | He thinks it is a cat
test: What would happen if you reached straight over the head of a dog you did not know? | It might feel threatened and duck, back off or snap | It would always wag and lick | It would fall asleep | It would roll over at once
test: «his fur's all spiky on his back, he's gonna kill us» What is closer to the truth? | Raised hackles mean he is very worked up, not always angry | It always means an attack is coming | It means he is cold | It means he just had a wash
test: Count them: three dogs do play bows at once in the park. How many front legs are on the grass? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: Count them: two dogs and one cat are on the sofa. How many tails and how many ears? | 3 tails and 6 ears | 2 tails and 6 ears | 3 tails and 3 ears | 3 tails and 12 ears
test: «your dog's well cute, can I stroke him?» What should happen next? | The owner says yes, the dog sniffs a hand, then a stroke on its side | Grab the dog round the neck for a big hug | Pat it hard on the head several times | Pull its ears gently and tickle under its chin until it rolls over
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a wagging tail in the domestic dog? | Its meaning depends upon the manner of the wag and the posture of the whole animal | It invariably signifies contentment | It invariably signifies aggression | It possesses no communicative significance whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the punishment of a growling dog is to be avoided. | The animal may thereby learn to bite without prior warning | Growling is invariably a symptom of disease of the throat or the teeth | Punishment renders the dog incapable of barking | Growling constitutes an expression of gratitude
exam: It is given that an unfamiliar dog approaches a child at speed in a public park. Ascertain the most appropriate course of action. | The child should remain motionless, arms withdrawn, and avert the gaze | The child should flee at the greatest possible speed towards the nearest gate | The child should shout loudly and wave both arms above the head | The child should fix the animal with a steady stare
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a dog which exhibits the whites of its eyes whilst embraced. | The animal is uncomfortable and ought to be released | The animal is expressing profound affection for the child who holds it | The animal is about to fall asleep | The animal is seeking a toy
